+Review and fix run in rounds. Answer every finding with a fix or, in the progress comment, a reason that cites evidence the reviewer could check: a file and line, a test, or a decision recorded in an issue or ADR. "It was intentional" is not evidence, and neither is the wish to defend code you wrote. Without such evidence, fix the finding or ask the human. Fix the finding's whole class. After a round with fixes, review only the new diff. After three rounds, step back: reconsider the approach and ask the human before a fourth.
+
+A round is clean when its review raises no finding, other than a nit, that is not already answered. Review is done after two clean rounds in a row, because a fresh review can find what the last one missed: after a clean round, review the whole PR diff again. If a new finding is in code that an earlier fix in this PR changed, and undoing that fix would resolve it, the fixes are going in circles: stop, record it in the progress comment, and ask the human which way to go.
 
 The issue is the source of truth across compaction, resumes and subagents: when memory and the issue disagree, reread the issue.
 
